1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,560
Eight a.m.

2
00:00:01,710 --> 00:00:03,770
A message from the research team.

3
00:00:03,790 --> 00:00:06,090
Was last night's run really offline?

4
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:08,170
And can we trust the result?

5
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:09,930
Here is the whole picture.

6
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:12,410
On the connected side, GitLab prepares

7
00:00:12,430 --> 00:00:14,090
everything the workflow needs.

8
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:14,840
Once.

9
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:16,330
It lands in private

10
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:18,010
AWS storage.

11
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:20,010
Every file is fingerprinted.

12
00:00:20,030 --> 00:00:22,250
Every container image is locked to

13
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:23,850
its exact identity.

14
00:00:23,870 --> 00:00:25,690
Then the run happens here, on

15
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:27,370
a host with no route to

16
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:28,410
the internet.

17
00:00:28,430 --> 00:00:31,050
Only private AWS endpoints can reach

18
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:31,720
it.

19
00:00:31,790 --> 00:00:33,130
So what if a task tries

20
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:33,930
to reach out?

21
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:35,290
There is nowhere to go.

22
00:00:35,310 --> 00:00:36,890
The run stops at stage eight,

23
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:38,090
and says FAIL.

24
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:38,970
Loudly.

25
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:40,650
Never silently.

26
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:41,530
And when you see

27
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:43,450
PASS, all ten checks passed,

28
00:00:43,470 --> 00:00:44,970
offline, with evidence.

29
00:00:44,990 --> 00:00:46,810
So you can tell the researcher:

30
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:47,370
yes.

31
00:00:47,390 --> 00:00:48,440
Here is the proof.
